WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.487
Configuration is part of the security boundary in ASP.

2
00:00:03.487 --> 00:00:04.779
NET Core.

3
00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:22.733
Appsettings files, environment variables, user secrets, command-line settings, deployment variables, cloud configuration, and external secret stores can affect identity, cookies, logging, Data Protection, database access, feature flags, CORS, diagnostics, and endpoint behavior.

4
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:27.770
A configuration mistake can change the security posture without a code change.

5
00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:33.001
Development secret storage is not the same as production-grade secret protection.

6
00:00:33.001 --> 00:00:40.170
User secrets can keep local development values out of source files, but they are not a production secret store.

7
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:53.861
Production secrets need managed storage, access control, rotation, auditability, operational ownership, and deployment practices that keep secret values away from repositories, logs, tickets, and build artifacts.

8
00:00:53.861 --> 00:00:57.090
Secrets should not be committed to source control.

9
00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:03.096
Repositories are copied, cached, mirrored, reviewed, backed up, and sometimes shared broadly.

10
00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:09.103
A secret committed once can remain in history even after it is removed from the current file.

11
00:01:09.103 --> 00:01:18.402
Configuration review should look for connection strings, API keys, tokens, certificates, signing material, and private endpoints before release.

12
00:01:18.402 --> 00:01:19.694
ASP.

13
00:01:19.694 --> 00:01:25.958
NET Core Data Protection provides services used to protect cookies, tokens, and application data.

14
00:01:25.958 --> 00:01:32.869
Conceptually, it uses managed keys so protected data can later be read by the application when appropriate.

15
00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:44.171
Teams do not need to invent their own protection mechanism for framework features, but they do need to understand where keys are stored and how they behave across deployments.

16
00:01:44.171 --> 00:01:47.658
Key persistence matters in multi-instance deployments.

17
00:01:47.658 --> 00:01:55.472
If each instance has incompatible keys, one instance may not read protected cookies, tokens, or data produced by another.

18
00:01:55.472 --> 00:02:03.997
Key rotation also needs planning so old protected data can remain readable for the required period while new data uses current keys.

19
00:02:03.997 --> 00:02:10.262
The right key storage depends on the hosting environment, but it should be explicit and reviewed.

20
00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:15.428
Configuration and key handling should be tested in production-like environments.

21
00:02:15.428 --> 00:02:22.016
Local behavior can hide problems because one developer machine is not the same as a scaled deployment.

22
00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:33.188
Review how settings are loaded, how missing settings fail, how secrets are referenced without being printed, and how Data Protection keys are shared, rotated, and protected.
